=== Complete Solutions

#sol-item(1, "C", dense: true)[
  Carbon dioxide and water are the raw materials; glucose and oxygen are the products.
]

#sol-item(2, "C", dense: true)[
  Palisade cells are packed with chloroplasts and lie near the upper surface where the light is strongest.
]

#sol-item(3, "D", dense: true)[
  Removing the green pigment lets the blue-black colour of the iodine-starch reaction be seen.
]

#sol-item(4, "D", dense: true)[
  When light is no longer limiting, another factor — carbon dioxide or temperature — controls the rate.
]

#sol-item(5, "C", dense: true)[
  Chloroplasts contain the chlorophyll and the enzymes for photosynthesis.
]

#sol-item(6, "B", dense: true)[
  Oxygen is released when water is split in the light-dependent reaction.
]

#sol-item(7, "B", dense: true)[
  The white areas lack chlorophyll, so comparing them with the green areas shows that chlorophyll is needed.
]

#sol-item(8, "D", dense: true)[
  The cuticle is waterproof and transparent — it reduces evaporation without blocking light.
]

#sol-item(9, "C", dense: true)[
  Chlorophyll absorbs mainly red and blue light and reflects green, which is why leaves look green.
]

#sol-item(10, "A", dense: true)[
  Carbon dioxide from the air and water from the soil.
]

#sol-item(11, "A", dense: true)[
  Plants store carbohydrate as starch; glycogen is the animal storage form.
]

#sol-item(12, "B", dense: true)[
  Closed stomata cut off the carbon dioxide supply, making it the limiting factor.
]

#sol-item(13, "B", dense: true)[
  They form a large internal surface over which carbon dioxide and oxygen can diffuse.
]

#sol-item(14, "B", dense: true)[
  A colourless, chloroplast-free epidermis transmits maximum light to the palisade layer.
]

#sol-item(15, "A", dense: true)[
  Light energy is trapped by chlorophyll and stored as chemical energy in glucose.
]

#sol-item(16, "B", dense: true)[
  Magnesium sits at the centre of the chlorophyll molecule; iron is needed for its manufacture but is not part of it.
]

#sol-item(17, "D", dense: true)[
  In darkness the starch in the leaves is used up, so the leaf is destarched — the standard first step in photosynthesis experiments.
]

#sol-item(18, "D", dense: true)[
  In a sealed jar the carbon dioxide is used up quickly, so it becomes the limiting factor.
]

#sol-item(19, "B", dense: true)[
  Water is carried up from the roots in the xylem of the veins.
]

#sol-item(20, "D", dense: true)[
  Iodine turns blue-black in the presence of starch.
]

#sol-item(21, "B", dense: true)[
  Haemoglobin is an animal pigment; the others are all genuine plant fates of glucose.
]

#sol-item(22, "A", dense: true)[
  Without light, the light-dependent stage stops and the whole process halts.
]

#sol-item(23, "B", dense: true)[
  At the compensation point, the carbon dioxide taken in equals that released, so the net exchange is zero.
]

#sol-item(24, "C", dense: true)[
  A thin leaf gives a short diffusion path for carbon dioxide and oxygen.
]

#sol-item(25, "C", dense: true)[
  Each vascular bundle contains xylem bringing water and phloem removing sugar.
]

#sol-item(26, "C", dense: true)[
  Nitrogen is needed for amino acids, proteins and chlorophyll, so its shortage stunts growth and yellows the leaves.
]

#sol-item(27, "D", dense: true)[
  Photolysis of water in the light-dependent reaction releases oxygen.
]

#sol-item(28, "A", dense: true)[
  Photosynthesis needs light; respiration continues day and night in every living cell.
]

#sol-item(29, "D", dense: true)[
  Stomata, controlled by guard cells, are the openings through which gases pass.
]

#sol-item(30, "C", dense: true)[
  Most stomata are on the lower surface, so coating it blocks gas exchange.
]

